oudy) and 4 (estimated from the historical record) excluded."},"min_cell_fraction":0.5,"source":{"id":"mod13c2","short_name":"NASA MODIS/Terra vegetation index (MOD13C2 v6.1)","dataset":"MODIS/Terra Vegetation Indices Monthly L3 Global 0.05Deg CMG, Version 6.1 (MOD13C2)","producer":"NASA MODIS Land Science Team; distributed by NASA LP DAAC","access":"NASA LP DAAC (Earthdata); regional means computed by the team","access_short":"NASA LP DAAC","access_url":"https://doi.org/10.5067/MODIS/MOD13C2.061","doi":"10.5067/MODIS/MOD13C2.061","native_grid":"0.05\u00b0 latitude x 0.05\u00b0 longitude (climate modeling grid)","kind":"Satellite observation: a vegetation index computed from red and near-infrared reflectance measured by the MODIS instrument on NASA's Terra satellite.","caveat":"NDVI is a satellite measurement of greenness, not a direct measure of plant health or biomass. Clouds, snow, smoke and low sun angles lower it, and it saturates over dense forest. The record starts in 2000, so it is shorter than the 1981-2025 weather variables and trends are harder to detect. The Terra satellite's overpass time has drifted earlier since 2020, which NASA reports is affecting recent data, so treat changes after about 2022 with extra care.","credit":"NASA MODIS/Terra MOD13C2 v6.1 via NASA LP DAAC"}}}